[Music]

4
00:00:19,279 --> 00:00:21,830
This is Sam Tagert for SE radio.

5
00:00:21,830 --> 00:00:26,150
I am here today with Dan Berg Johnson and Daniel Jurgen.

6
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:29,429
Daniel Jurgen is a frequent conference speaker on cyber security.

7
00:00:29,429 --> 00:00:36,150
He's worked on cyber security in a wide variety of domains and is currently the chief academy officer for Omega point in Sweden.

8
00:00:36,150 --> 00:00:43,270
Dan Berg Johnson is a partner at Omega Point and also a frequent conference speaker along with another Dan Dan Sano.

9
00:00:43,270 --> 00:00:45,430
Dan and Daniel authored a book called Secure by Design.

10
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:54,389
We've talked previously about security in many episodes such as 584 Charles Weir on ruthless security for busy developers.

11
00:00:54,389 --> 00:01:06,150
453 Aaron Reinhardt on security chaos engineering 418 Vladimir Cororicov on functional programming in enterprise applications and 405 Yughenei Brickman on infrastructure as code best practices. Welcome Dan and Daniel.

12
00:01:06,159 --> 00:01:07,670
Hey, thanks. Thank you very much.

13
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:11,350
Thanks for having us. Thank you.

14
00:01:11,350 --> 00:01:13,830
Let's start by talking about your book.

15
00:01:13,830 --> 00:01:24,789
My main takeaway from the book was that instead of training engineers to be security professionals, it is easier to emphasize good design practices and that those good design practices just so happen to address a lot of security concerns as well.

16
00:01:24,789 --> 00:01:26,789
Is that the intended takeaway?

17
00:01:26,799 --> 00:01:37,749
Yeah, I would say the purpose when we wrote the book was that as engineers, as developers, you're so focused on delivering new features, keeping the timelines.

18
00:01:37,749 --> 00:01:43,670
You have a tech lead or some sort of product lead that's on your back all the time.

19
00:01:43,670 --> 00:01:47,590
So keeping your focus on security as well that becomes a great burden or what do you say Dan?

20
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:53,670
Yeah I would say captures 95% of the intended meaning.

21
00:01:53,670 --> 00:02:05,670
You get a lot of security from focusing on good design patterns which as Daniel alluded to is easier for developers to just keep at the back of their head all the time than explicitly thinking about security.

22
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:16,390
Whereas there is a rest 5% that you really should be aware of security practices and special procedures as well.

23
00:02:16,390 --> 00:02:23,670
So you can't ignore them but definitely I think it kept 95% of the intent in that.

24
00:02:23,670 --> 00:02:37,750
So in general as a developer you would then start writing secure software simply by applying good design practices and get that security I wouldn't say for free but for a lot cheaper than you would do otherwise.

25
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:52,229
So that brings me to a question is there some baseline level of generic security awareness training that you recommend for developers or is your recommendation more just let developers focus on good design?

26
00:02:52,229 --> 00:03:02,390
I think there's to be able to have this kinds of ideas that secure by design is around that you should use design patterns in a deliberate way to get improvement in security.

27
00:03:02,390 --> 00:03:06,869
You need to have a basic awareness of what things you are addressing.

28
00:03:06,869 --> 00:03:13,110
For example, there's a lot of practices to protect yourself against crosshair scripting or SQL injection stuff like that.

29
00:03:13,110 --> 00:03:21,750
But if you don't know about SQL injection at all, you don't really know what good designs to look for.

30
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:32,149
So familiarity with quite a breadth of security things, but you don't need that kind of like specialized knowledge to get a lot of lots of benefit from that knowledge.

31
00:03:32,159 --> 00:03:38,149
Can you describe the traditional approach to security and some of the problems with it?

32
00:03:38,149 --> 00:03:50,630
I would say one of the challenges with the traditional view is that you have to almost be an expert to understand what to do and if you misconfigure something could be a framework it could be a library or whatnot you might miss something and if

33
00:03:50,640 --> 00:04:05,350
you make that mistake suddenly you're vulnerable or you allow something and that's a big problem for many developers that they don't feel educated enough on that topic in order to make those type of decisions.

34
00:04:05,350 --> 00:04:19,590
Yeah, we often see that there's kind of like a gap traditionally between traditional development role and traditional security role which is hurtful actually because it makes developers shy away from security

35
00:04:19,590 --> 00:04:27,110
and say that oh we have met really senior architects that say oh I take responsibility

36
00:04:27,110 --> 00:04:35,510
for the uptime of the system I take responsible for the maintainability of the system or I take responsible for this and this and that but

37
00:04:35,510 --> 00:04:42,390
when it comes to security they go oh security no I'm not a security there are specialists that will take care of that which does not really help at all

38
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:50,710
and the fact that you have that mindset points to an organization where you have us

39
00:04:50,710 --> 00:04:58,070
and them that security is somebody else's problem it's not really your thing but in reality security is a concern

40
00:04:58,070 --> 00:05:07,110
you need to design your software in a way that is secure in every aspect so to say and we have seen a similar play play out before security.

41
00:05:07,110 --> 00:05:15,909
If we look at the early days of agile with this gulf between developers that is coders,

42
00:05:15,909 --> 00:05:29,830
programmers and testers where testing was a very separate activity and the collaboration between developers and testers were not always hard but perhaps even like antagonistic and it didn't help either camp.

43
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:38,390
And throughout a few years we have learned to include coding and testing into the same cycle.

44
00:05:38,390 --> 00:05:44,070
And I think we see the same kind of progress now that security has been a very separate field.

45
00:05:44,070 --> 00:05:58,390
But now security is embraced within coding and testing as a part of as Daniel said one of many concerns and a really important concern but none that should be treated separately.

46
00:05:58,390 --> 00:06:01,990
Writing secure software today is more of an hygiene.

47
00:06:01,990 --> 00:06:09,110
I would say it is an expectancy nowadays of almost any client you interact with, they expect the software to be secure.

48
00:06:09,110 --> 00:06:17,830
I mean, if you design something that's insecure, it's not going to pass as good software.

49
00:06:17,830 --> 00:06:26,790
So, having the skill to apply the certain patterns, have the mindset and so forth, that becomes something that everybody needs to do now and it's so important because of that.

50
00:06:26,790 --> 00:06:36,629
So your comment on agile and developers and testers before you came up with that I was thinking of the DevOps idea of combining developers and operations which is also very similar.

51
00:06:36,629 --> 00:06:38,870
So there seems to be a theme there.

52
00:06:38,870 --> 00:06:42,870
Definitely I see that these are the three very consecutive steps.

53
00:06:42,870 --> 00:06:53,430
First developers embracing testing and testers getting so get a dev test culture and then we've got the DevOps culture and now we've got the security.

54
00:06:53,430 --> 00:06:57,350
So we're rolling more and more roles into this fruitful collaboration.

55
00:06:57,360 --> 00:07:01,990
So do you still see separate roles for security practitioners as well?

56
00:07:01,990 --> 00:07:08,470
Is it about combining development and security together or is it about both overlapping a little bit? How does that work?

57
00:07:08,479 --> 00:07:12,230
I would say that you can compare this to like a physician, right?

58
00:07:12,230 --> 00:07:21,749
I mean you have experts those that are really good at doing surgery or something and then you have the common practitioner that sort of has a broad understanding.

59
00:07:21,749 --> 00:07:29,909
Most developers will be those have a broad understanding whereas you will still have experts in certain areas right I mean

60
00:07:29,909 --> 00:07:38,390
you could be an expert in authentication authorization login flows stuff like that right whereas some others might be experts in cryptoology

61
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:52,710
and stuff like that and if we jump the fence a little bit and put oursel on the other side and ask the dual question to the security professionals they say that of Of course,

62
00:07:52,710 --> 00:08:00,790
we have got experts that are like really good offensive security testers that can try to get into your systems,

63
00:08:00,790 --> 00:08:08,950
but those experts become a lot better if they know a little bit of coding or if they know a little bit about infrastructure or CIS admin or whatever.

64
00:08:08,950 --> 00:08:23,749
So I think we're seeing the same thing on the both side of the fence that we will both retain our deep professional knowledge and expertise but we will start to learn a little bit more about each others in the corporation will get a lot better.

65
00:08:23,749 --> 00:08:28,469
The book specifically talks about domain driven design.

66
00:08:28,469 --> 00:08:40,149
I realize that's a very large topic with a lot of books written about it, but can you briefly describe the main ideas behind domain driven design, the ones that at least apply to security?

67
00:08:40,149 --> 00:08:51,350
Well, if we pick out the part that we think give us the most leverage, it is the message of domain driven design that a conceptual idea of what you are building is really essential.

68
00:08:51,350 --> 00:08:56,389
A application or system is not just a lot of code that happens to work.

69
00:08:56,389 --> 00:09:01,750
It's kind of like encapsulate and encode a certain set of ideas and how they work together.

70
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:10,710
So if you work on a finance system, you should know what is an account in this kind of context as opposed from a transaction which is something else.

71
00:09:10,710 --> 00:09:19,430
And they might in different solutions get together and have a little bit different meanings and different boundaries between them.

72
00:09:19,430 --> 00:09:26,310
But for this precise application we're building, we need to have a sharp understanding of what we mean with the different things.

73
00:09:26,310 --> 00:09:38,470
And that kind of deep understanding which eliminates a lot of misconceptions and thus bugs is what we built the security upon.

74
00:09:38,470 --> 00:09:44,949
As you say there, the specificity of domain driven design and knowing exactly what you need is what you can also use from a security aspect.

75
00:09:44,949 --> 00:09:55,509
One of the most common mistakes that are done is that you use two, how should I say, generic or broad definitions of your data types in your software.

76
00:09:55,509 --> 00:09:59,910
So if you instead choose something that's very specific,

77
00:09:59,910 --> 00:10:08,070
then you can narrow the input to only match that definition and then it becomes very difficult to inject something that violates that contract.

78
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:20,069
For example, if you're inputting an address, a zip code throughout the world might look almost in a conceivable format.

79
00:10:20,069 --> 00:10:27,269
But if you're just writing a software for France or for US or for some other limited domain,

80
00:10:27,269 --> 00:10:37,430
well then you can narrow it down very very harshly and get a tight definition of it which makes injection attacks a bit more difficult to succeed.

81
00:10:37,430 --> 00:10:40,550
Yes, there are several different directions we could go with this.

82
00:10:40,550 --> 00:10:42,310
You hit on several things that I want to talk about.

83
00:10:42,310 --> 00:10:49,030
The first thing I think that's worth talking about is domain language because I think that goes to your specificity part.

84
00:10:49,030 --> 00:10:54,790
I remember working on a system where we had both order numbers and order ids and oh that sounds fun.

85
00:10:54,800 --> 00:11:03,269
Trying to remember which one was which and when to use which one was quite difficult and we didn't really have a good dictionary of all the terms.

86
00:11:03,269 --> 00:11:08,630
So on a project would you have like a domain dictionary that says these are what each thing represents?

87
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:13,350
I guess a dictionary but what you would have I guess is a model right?

88
00:11:13,350 --> 00:11:21,269
I mean you have a conceptual model where your terms are defined so to say and the relationship between these terms.

89
00:11:21,269 --> 00:11:30,150
So that is what you should have and we encourage everyone to define their ubiquitous language as we talk in domain driven design

90
00:11:30,150 --> 00:11:41,430
right to have one definition of the word and no synonyms and so forth and that helps you to understand exactly what you need to fulfill for a certain concept in your domain.

91
00:11:41,430 --> 00:11:45,590
So in that sense we have a dictionary but it's expressed mostly like a model.

92
00:11:45,590 --> 00:11:52,470
I would say I would usually say that the dictionary is an excellent starting point for building a model.

93
00:11:52,470 --> 00:12:00,710
If you haven't got the dictionary, you should definitely start there and collect not all of the words that you use.

94
00:12:00,710 --> 00:12:07,910
But ensure to really well define some 15 or 20 that are really important terms.

95
00:12:07,910 --> 00:12:12,230
But as Daniel said, a language does not stop with the dictionary.

96
00:12:12,230 --> 00:12:14,710
It's also how do you construct a sentence?

97
00:12:14,710 --> 00:12:17,269
like what verbs and nouns go well together.

98
00:12:17,269 --> 00:12:31,350
Do you say that you register an order or do you say that you put a purchase request or do you say that you get a warrant form refill formulation or whatever.

99
00:12:31,350 --> 00:12:41,430
So watching out to see that have we got a lot of synonyms that there might not be precisely synonyms and then in that case try to prune your language.

100
00:12:41,430 --> 00:12:46,389
You also get the idiomatic what kind of phrasings go well together.

101
00:12:46,389 --> 00:12:59,750
And I think there's a lot to do with that that you really need to speak that language out in the open because then you'll catch yourself when you start drifting off that terminology and start using other words.

102
00:12:59,750 --> 00:13:07,590
That's a sign that here you got something that you need to specify a little bit better because sooner or later you'll have to write it in code.

103
00:13:07,590 --> 00:13:13,269
And if you can't speak it clearly, you're in a worse position when it comes to coding.

104
00:13:13,269 --> 00:13:20,230
So yes, the diction is a starting point, but it's more about like books and literatures and how you express yourself in sentences.

105
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:27,190
I would also like to add that having your language shouldn't just be written down in a model or as a dictionary.

106
00:13:27,190 --> 00:13:35,350
It should also be reflected in your requirements, in your tests and so forth and in your code, in your code, in your UX.

107
00:13:35,360 --> 00:13:36,949
Yeah.

108
00:13:36,959 --> 00:13:43,350
So it is truly a ubiquitous language that you have created for your context where you you operate.

109
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:49,110
Do you have any specific examples you've seen where miscommunication contributed to a security issue?

110
00:13:49,120 --> 00:14:00,790
So you see it this way you have a shopping cart and in that shopping cart you can add items or products books perhaps like four copies of secure by design.

111
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:03,350
Yeah.

112
00:14:03,350 --> 00:14:09,269
So four copies of secure by design we say you have a quantity of four right so quantity is just an integer.

113
00:14:09,269 --> 00:14:13,269
Yeah, the problem now is that if you have defined quantity as an integer,

114
00:14:13,269 --> 00:14:21,269
what you'll automatically accept in your software is all the capabilities of an integer.

115
00:14:21,269 --> 00:14:26,790
It can be added, it can be negative and it can be extremely big, right?

116
00:14:26,790 --> 00:14:36,150
But from a business perspective, I assume you won't accept a negative quantity of books or a billion quantity of books, right?

117
00:14:36,150 --> 00:14:48,069
I mean it doesn't make sense from the business perspective but from the software perspective that works since you used an integer and we've seen this is really there have been some really public bugs around this

118
00:14:48,079 --> 00:14:57,509
where a negative amount multiplied by a positive price ends up as a negative contribution

119
00:14:57,509 --> 00:15:09,590
to the total sum basically giving a discount and this has been both in public main and we've seen it over and over and over again in our assessments where we've built into customers and clients and worked with them.

120
00:15:09,590 --> 00:15:17,269
So you mean that I can create myself a discount by just adding minus 10 of order number blah blah blah. Oh that was not intended.

121
00:15:17,279 --> 00:15:23,509
It is a discount feature I would say but but it's probably not what the business would like.

122
00:15:23,509 --> 00:15:33,509
The interesting part if you analyze this from a why does this happen and usually it is developers tendency to fall back on language primitives.

123
00:15:33,519 --> 00:15:40,550
language primitive types that you have either be a string, an integer, a float or something like that.

124
00:15:40,550 --> 00:15:53,590
So they use that to represent a very specific concept in your business domain but you use a generic concept to do that specific work.

125
00:15:53,590 --> 00:15:59,749
So string is also very common that you accept a string but you expected it to be an order number or what you mentioned before.

126
00:15:59,749 --> 00:16:09,269
Now we're talking about just the range of sensible quantities like from one or zero up to 200 something or whatever it is.

127
00:16:09,269 --> 00:16:13,430
But it might also be the operations you can do on them.

128
00:16:13,430 --> 00:16:18,310
For example, we have had a client where they let's obuscate this a little bit to protect them.

129
00:16:18,310 --> 00:16:28,949
They had bus numbers and at one point there was an addition where one bus number was added to another bus number and of course that was not the intent.

130
00:16:28,949 --> 00:16:35,910
It was two other things, but someone had just mistaken two parameters for each other and ended up adding two bus numbers,

131
00:16:35,910 --> 00:16:46,150
coming up with something that looked like a bus number, but in the end made no sense at all and led to very strange behavior a few steps down the line.

132
00:16:46,150 --> 00:16:47,910
Also, something that is preventable.

133
00:16:47,910 --> 00:16:52,069
You shouldn't be able to add two bus numbers to each other.

134
00:16:52,069 --> 00:17:00,790
Yeah, I think the other place I've seen this is in something that maybe takes in two integers, but they represent two different things and it's very easy to swap them if you're not careful.

135
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:07,510
And especially in like a strictly type language, if you swap them, then hopefully the compiler would catch that and break that if they were different types.

136
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:14,150
Like longitude and latitude just happened because which is longer and which is lat.

137
00:17:14,150 --> 00:17:16,870
No one ever remembers that.

138
00:17:16,870 --> 00:17:30,230
That's also some more intricate stuffs like we were talking about some people in the gambling online gambling industry where they hand out money like for free spins and stuff like that and that is represented as dollars.

139
00:17:30,230 --> 00:17:40,230
But then people are very good at finding ways of registering something, get an amount of dollars free and then withdraw them.

140
00:17:40,230 --> 00:17:51,909
So often they end up adding a lot of very strange business rules about what dollars you can withdraw after having this or that much gambling.

141
00:17:51,909 --> 00:17:58,711
And at one place which we discussed with they were actually doing explicit modeling of that. So they didn't have money.

142
00:17:58,711 --> 00:18:01,190
They had withdrawable money.

143
00:18:01,190 --> 00:18:03,350
They had deposit money.

144
00:18:03,350 --> 00:18:08,390
They had free spin money and then they had rules for how to convert them to each other.

145
00:18:08,390 --> 00:18:13,909
So there's a good example where they actively had made a modeling decision to separate different things.

146
00:18:13,919 --> 00:18:27,510
I think it all comes down to if you are careful and start modeling your concepts and add how should I say specific rules to it then it becomes much much harder

147
00:18:27,520 --> 00:18:37,111
to inject something that violates that like we see for every injection attack that you inject something that the software didn't expect. Right?

148
00:18:37,111 --> 00:18:44,231
So you can access the database and it behaves in a way you didn't anticipate and so forth, right? So yeah, be specific.

149
00:18:44,240 --> 00:18:48,630
That's our rule. Either inject or to confuse the state.

150
00:18:48,640 --> 00:18:59,830
Yeah. That you end up in a state that was not anticipated and not desired but enabled the user to do stuff that they shouldn't be able to do.

151
00:18:59,830 --> 00:19:03,750
We've been talking around the idea of domain objects which I don't think we actually specified.

152
00:19:03,750 --> 00:19:08,310
So in domain driven design this idea of domain objects. Do you want to just say what that is?

153
00:19:08,320 --> 00:19:16,310
So a domain object is most likely a concept that's native in that particular domain, right?

154
00:19:16,310 --> 00:19:21,350
I mean, if you talk about hospitality, then of course room number is very common.

155
00:19:21,350 --> 00:19:26,870
If you're at a hotel and you say, "Hey, put this on my room and things like that." That's a concept that needs to be expressed in that domain.

156
00:19:26,870 --> 00:19:32,789
So a domain object is something that's very familiar in that context.

157
00:19:32,789 --> 00:19:44,789
I think it might be valuable also to point out that we in modeling often see these domain objects coming out in two main flavors modeling wise.

158
00:19:44,789 --> 00:19:55,110
The most usual that people tend to be familiar with from object orientation basic 101 at universities and stuff like that are of course the entities.

159
00:19:55,110 --> 00:20:04,710
It's something that concept that models something which has got a lifespan and an identity that can be changed.

160
00:20:04,710 --> 00:20:06,710
So a stay at an hotel is something that might be prolonged.

161
00:20:06,710 --> 00:20:11,669
It might have a tab associated with it which can grow.

162
00:20:11,669 --> 00:20:22,870
A person might be something that who can change their names and grow older or change their relationships and states in different ways.

163
00:20:22,870 --> 00:20:34,149
The other big modeling tool is of course the value object which models a specific aspect of something like a color red

164
00:20:34,149 --> 00:20:37,909
or the number four or the duration of 24 hours.

165
00:20:37,919 --> 00:20:47,190
So it's a little bit more like writing something down on piece of paper and then you can be able to share it but you will not change it.

166
00:20:47,190 --> 00:20:53,350
A duration of 24 hours cannot be 25 hours because then it's another duration.

167
00:20:53,350 --> 00:20:57,110
So those two tools are very fruitful to keep apart.

168
00:20:57,120 --> 00:21:05,909
So want to make sure I understand correctly when you're talking about value objects generally they are immutable. Would that be a correct term to use?

169
00:21:05,919 --> 00:21:07,750
Absolutely.

170
00:21:07,750 --> 00:21:10,789
A value object that is not immutable would be a very strange thing.

171
00:21:10,799 --> 00:21:13,990
The next questions I have are around validations.

172
00:21:13,990 --> 00:21:19,909
But something occurred to me while you were talking, Daniel, about room numbers and oh, I want to charge something to my room.

173
00:21:19,909 --> 00:21:23,430
There seems to me there actually are two validations there.

174
00:21:23,430 --> 00:21:27,590
One is that it's actually a valid room number, but the other one is that there's actually somebody staying in that room.

175
00:21:27,600 --> 00:21:38,870
Yes, exactly. And so in the book, y'all make a very strong point about the order in which you validate stuff and how that contributes to security. Can you talk a little bit about that?

176
00:21:38,880 --> 00:21:41,430
Yeah.

177
00:21:41,430 --> 00:21:50,549
So the general principle here is that if you look at the relationship between an attacker and the attackee I guess then you want the relationship to be asymmetric in the sense

178
00:21:50,549 --> 00:22:05,029
that it should be really expensive for the attacker to craft the malicious input and it should be really cheap for the the recipient the server right so we should be able to reject something quickly and at a low cost.

179
00:22:05,029 --> 00:22:10,310
So because of that we've just created what we call an order of validation.

180
00:22:10,310 --> 00:22:19,830
Basically it's a five-step order where we do the cheapest operations first and well the first operation to check that would guess be origin.

181
00:22:19,830 --> 00:22:30,310
Does this come from a valid place it could be that it does come from something that you trust or that it has correct token or something like that that it's it's actually comes from a valid origin.

