when the auditors come in and say we want to have a look at that we can see what's happened in your systems the last seven years

362
00:44:50,710 --> 00:44:59,589
and by doing what Daniel said you can get that separations so you can treat each of those log syncs

363
00:44:59,589 --> 00:45:13,349
for different purposes separate you can also make your CFO very happy because then of course you can have much cheaper solution for your metrics compared to your audit log right that you need to maintain for years whereas the metrics can go away tomorrow, right?

364
00:45:13,359 --> 00:45:15,589
I mean, doesn't really matter.

365
00:45:15,589 --> 00:45:17,910
So, that also comes from using that type of design,

366
00:45:17,920 --> 00:45:30,950
but we think that log systems are so interesting and have so many concerns in and of themselves that we think that developers spontaneously don't take them serious enough.

367
00:45:30,950 --> 00:45:38,870
To a lot of developers, the log is just a file on your log system, but it's actually a subsystem of its own,

368
00:45:38,870 --> 00:45:45,190
which is own characteristics and runtime environments and financials implications and compliance implications.

369
00:45:45,190 --> 00:45:57,670
So, we think it's really worth to spell that out and make the logging a first order citizen subsystem and it merits an API of its own.

370
00:45:57,670 --> 00:46:07,829
It maint it needs management of its own etc. Have you found that the recent log for JBug brought logging to more people's attention at least in the seauite perhaps?

371
00:46:07,829 --> 00:46:19,990
Well, guilty as charge me personally definitely I'm quite surprised when when I talked to many people around that time many treated you know log forj as console output almost

372
00:46:19,990 --> 00:46:27,990
right I mean like if I input something I expect that something to be output

373
00:46:27,990 --> 00:46:37,270
you know and didn't see log forj as a I would say a library or a a thing that was its own system it was a handy way to write to the long file.

374
00:46:37,280 --> 00:46:39,430
Yeah, exactly.

375
00:46:39,430 --> 00:46:45,670
It was just a bridge to that and they expected data to be output exact the same way as you input.

376
00:46:45,670 --> 00:46:49,750
I think that sort of at least put logging on the agenda.

377
00:46:49,760 --> 00:47:04,790
Yeah. Yeah. I would say to to be honest, we actually had a few presentations at conferences thereafter under titles like what log forj the bal taught us about secure by design and and stuff like that.

378
00:47:04,790 --> 00:47:11,430
There is one more topic I want to talk about briefly and that is the three Rs that uh feature in your book. Can you talk about that a little bit?

379
00:47:11,440 --> 00:47:14,230
Sure.

380
00:47:14,230 --> 00:47:18,470
The three Rs stand for rotate, repave and repair. Dan, so what's your favorite R here?

381
00:47:18,480 --> 00:47:25,270
I think we just briefly should just do the same thing and say where is this design?

382
00:47:25,270 --> 00:47:28,150
Where does it come from and where does it end up security-wise?

383
00:47:28,160 --> 00:47:37,270
So basically this takes springboard off work in the reliability field for cloud

384
00:47:37,280 --> 00:47:48,309
for example the 12 factor manifesto the rugged manifesto etc and is a realm of designs

385
00:47:48,309 --> 00:47:58,711
that make things easier for you to survive in a cloud environment but also comes with interesting security benefits. So the three Rs,

386
00:47:58,711 --> 00:48:10,470
my favorite I would say is the one that is strangely named repave but which I understand as replace

387
00:48:10,470 --> 00:48:16,550
and is a routine of replacing your running instances in a structured and scheduled manner.

388
00:48:16,550 --> 00:48:24,870
For example, if you have a cluster of five nodes, then you spin up a new one, commission new one,

389
00:48:24,870 --> 00:48:32,549
and then decommission another one just because it's an hour old, not because it's broken or something like that.

390
00:48:32,549 --> 00:48:38,390
So, you constantly replace everything that you got on your cluster with fresh instances.

391
00:48:38,390 --> 00:48:40,309
from a security aspect.

392
00:48:40,309 --> 00:48:49,990
What that means is that if you're as an attacker able to install something on those nodes, then you have to reinstall every time a new image is commissioned into your cluster.

393
00:48:49,990 --> 00:48:52,950
And then you might say, oh well that shouldn't be too bad for an attacker.

394
00:48:52,950 --> 00:48:59,109
They could probably script this or, you know, keep installing their stuff on on those nodes.

395
00:48:59,109 --> 00:49:01,270
But what that also then creates is traffic on your network, right?

396
00:49:01,270 --> 00:49:05,829
I mean, it increases the chances of detecting this type of activity.

397
00:49:05,829 --> 00:49:14,950
So that's only one or by say basically let's in a random fashion we could repave our cluster with you know our images

398
00:49:14,950 --> 00:49:27,349
for instance if if that's the case and the kind of threat actors that we want to make life difficult for it's not the script kid that do drive by hacking because they're not that dangerous those

399
00:49:27,349 --> 00:49:35,190
that we want to shield off in this kind of of schemes of course the kind of persistent threat actors that do advance persistent threats

400
00:49:35,200 --> 00:49:42,630
because they got patience and they got resources and they installed something and they use that as a sniffer.

401
00:49:42,630 --> 00:49:46,549
And by doing this kind of routine that Daniel talks about, they get lost.

402
00:49:46,549 --> 00:49:53,349
Everything they've installed is just get lost and they need to reinstall instead and then they increase the chances of getting detected.

403
00:49:53,359 --> 00:49:58,710
Another R is of course rotation and that's rotational secrets.

404
00:49:58,710 --> 00:50:11,270
For instance, by designing your software to get your secrets from your environment like a key vault or some other system that contains your secret.

405
00:50:11,270 --> 00:50:17,430
You could design it so that the secret is rotated on a I guess a random schedule.

406
00:50:17,430 --> 00:50:19,430
You can be really offensive if you want to.

407
00:50:19,430 --> 00:50:21,270
You can go by changing secrets after each call.

408
00:50:21,270 --> 00:50:23,190
I guess that would be crazy.

409
00:50:23,190 --> 00:50:27,430
But in theory, you can have a very very aggressive rotation scheme.

410
00:50:27,430 --> 00:50:35,910
I think this becomes a little bit clearer if you see the anti pattern that we are trying to antagonize against.

411
00:50:35,910 --> 00:50:41,190
Daniel, what is the the record holder we've seen of people having the same production database?

412
00:50:41,200 --> 00:50:47,190
I think at least the record I think you're referring to is one client.

413
00:50:47,190 --> 00:50:53,109
Well, they hadn't changed their password in production for the past 18 years.

414
00:50:53,109 --> 00:51:00,309
The downside was that they also used the same password in their other environments as well because it was much easier.

415
00:51:00,319 --> 00:51:08,630
My immediate question is how many employees left during 18 years who still knew that password 18 years aware that it was too many.

416
00:51:08,640 --> 00:51:10,630
Yeah. Yeah.

417
00:51:10,630 --> 00:51:24,470
And I think there's something really interesting because that talks indirectly to what we are talking about that the reason for not changing it for 18 years is what is it was too complicated to be able to get around that.

418
00:51:24,470 --> 00:51:30,790
You would basically need to have all the developers and all the operations people in the house in the same time.

419
00:51:30,790 --> 00:51:35,109
You had to tear down all the system, recompile everything from scratch and get it up and running again.

420
00:51:35,119 --> 00:51:42,470
So the application design was putting a stop to doing rotation of passwords and

421
00:51:42,480 --> 00:51:56,069
that is what secure by design of course is about that we should make designs that make the systems operationable so that it's possible to do these kind of things.

422
00:51:56,069 --> 00:52:07,750
So I would say that if you have designed your software that you either I mean I guess you you hardcode your secrets in your code or that you supply them at deploy time perhaps that could also be the case.

423
00:52:07,750 --> 00:52:15,829
Then you you create a mess that all your your dependent system on that secret needs to be either redeployed or recompiled,

424
00:52:15,839 --> 00:52:25,349
rebuilt, which makes it very difficult for someone to decide that oh we should change this password now or we should you know rotate this.

425
00:52:25,349 --> 00:52:31,750
Instead, if you design your software such way that you fetch the secret upon need,

426
00:52:31,750 --> 00:52:40,069
then you can simply rotate it in between those fetches and also create a how should I say a resilience retry mechanism against the resource

427
00:52:40,069 --> 00:52:48,069
that you're trying to access and make sure that well if my key or whatever I have doesn't work anymore, I need to go and fetch a new one.

428
00:52:48,069 --> 00:52:54,310
And that that sort of emulates the problem of a very flaky network or something like that, right? for service.

429
00:52:54,319 --> 00:53:05,990
I think it's interesting to see that this is also an instance where a lot of interlocking designs together give a very strong security benefit because under the hood

430
00:53:05,990 --> 00:53:15,109
for this to work you have got the designs of making your configuration or configuration parameters external

431
00:53:15,109 --> 00:53:23,750
which in its turn make it possible to make immutable builds where you actually have the same build artifact in the test environments and in production.

432
00:53:23,750 --> 00:53:29,750
ction environments and in multiple instances of the production environment which also gives a lot of other security benefits.

433
00:53:29,750 --> 00:53:43,349
But it's all these design together that makes it possible to have this kind of lighthanded possible to change database production password without even having downtime.

434
00:53:43,349 --> 00:53:47,510
So we need to wrap up here but I do have one last question that I wanted to talk to and actually you kind of changed my thinking about.

